      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on my thesis regarding heat-induced browning. Can you explain the specific roles that amino acids and reducing sugars play in the Maillard reaction? Also, I need to know specifically how pH levels influence the rate of the reaction.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sure, the Maillard reaction between amino acids and reducing sugars such as glucose leads to browning. Amino acids contribute a nitrogen source, while reducing sugars provide the necessary reducing power to aid the reaction. When these two compounds react, they form complex flavor and color compounds. Regarding pH levels, the Maillard reaction is generally faster at neutral to slightly alkaline pH values. Acidic conditions might slow down the reaction, especially compared to the reaction at higher pH values. This is because the initial step involving amino acid carbonylation is more favorable in environments with higher pH.",
