    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you go into a bit more detail about the specific intermediate stages of the reaction, like the formation of Amadori rearrangement products? I'm trying to map out the exact progression from the initial condensation to the final melanoidins.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Maillard reaction is a complex series of steps with many intermediates. Initially, an N-H group of an amino acid reacts with the carbonyl group of a reducing sugar, forming a Schiff base, which is then stabilized into an Amadori product. This is a key intermediate stage because the Amadori compounds are more reactive and can undergo further modifications. They can cyclize to form 1-amino-1-deoxy-2-keto-3-deoxytetrahydropyrimidine (DKTP), or they can undergo dehydration to form 5-hydroxymethyl-2-furaldehyde (HMF). These compounds and other intermediates continue to undergo a multitude of rearrangements, degradations, and condensations that eventually lead to the formation of large polymeric melanoidins, responsible for the final product's color and browning. Each intermediate has its own reaction pathway which depends on the specific amino acid and sugar involved, the temperature, and pH conditions.",
      "source": "target",
